# Why each declared setting exists. One claim per setting; the config lint fails on any setting that
# is declared and not claimed here.

port 10000
  Plano's own example LLM listener port (config/test_passthrough.yaml). Nothing in this harness
  depends on the number; it is Plano's default rather than one we chose.

path /v1/chat/completions
  The OpenAI ingress route the crate serves (70 references in crates/, against 14 for /v1/messages).

auth dummy
  Plano requires access_key on a model_provider that is not passthrough_auth. The mock accepts any
  credential, so this is a placeholder that satisfies Plano's own validation, not a secret.

launch.args: none
  The image's ENTRYPOINT is supervisord (Dockerfile), which accepts no positional arguments.
  `planoai up <config>` is the CLI's interface, not the container's - passing it made the container
  refuse to start with "positional arguments are not supported". Nothing needs to be passed: the
  config is mounted where the image already looks for it.

mount conf/config.yaml -> /app/plano_config.yaml (read-only)
  The path the image's config generator reads by default (cli/planoai/config_generator.py:205), so
  Plano is launched the same way as the other file-configured entrants - kong mounts one file to
  /kong/kong.yml, apisix two into its conf dir - rather than through an environment variable only
  this gateway would use. Read-only because the gateway has no reason to write to it, and a writable
  mount would let a run mutate the input the next run reads.

listeners[].type: model
  Selects the LLM gateway listener rather than the agent-orchestration listener. Measuring the agent
  listener would put Plano's router and an agent hop inside a number every other entrant reports
  without one.

model_providers[].base_url
  Points every upstream at the harness mock. This is the setting that makes Plano measurable here at
  all; without it the cells would be untestable, as they are for the entrants that pin the vendor host.

model_providers[].provider_interface
  Selects the egress dialect per upstream (openai / claude / gemini). This is what the egress axis of
  the matrix is measuring, so it has to be stated per provider rather than inferred from the model
  name.

tracing.random_sampling: 0
  Tracing off. It is observability work the other entrants are not doing in their measured path, and
  Plano's default of 100 would sample every request on the hot path.

PLANO_CONFIG_FILE=/etc/plano/config.yaml
  boot. The image's entrypoint is supervisord, which accepts no arguments, so the config path cannot
  be passed on the command line - the first attempt did and the container refused to start with
  "positional arguments are not supported: ['up', '/etc/plano/config.yaml']". Its config generator
  reads this variable (cli/planoai/config_generator.py:205) and defaults to /app/plano_config.yaml,
  a path our read-only mount deliberately does not occupy.

KNOWN, UNRESOLVED: Plano dies after the first cell, and this harness cannot yet say why.
  In both field runs of 2026-07-28 (engines dd26a54 and 8f2af5d) Plano served openai>openai
  completely - every metric, including the memory phase's cold restart, 66s of load at c=32 and the
  60s idle after it - and then the next two cells recorded "no connection to the gateway: Connection
  refused (os error 111)". The same shape twice, so it reproduces; the process is simply gone by the
  time the second cell is probed.

  What has been ruled out, each by measurement rather than by argument:
    - load, on its own: 1280 requests at c=32 locally left the container up and serving.
    - a slow restart: a cold start to first 200 is 6 seconds, well inside the readiness budget.
    - a container-name collision on relaunch: the harness's stop path is `docker rm -f`, so the
      name is free before the next launch.
    - a container memory cap: none is set; the box has 64 GB and Plano's steady state is ~1 GB.

  The likely mechanism, unproven: Plano's supervisord tears the whole container down when any
  managed child exits - observed directly when config generation failed earlier, where a single
  child's exit took envoy and brightstaff with it. If envoy or brightstaff dies under the streaming
  phases, the container exits and nothing inside it restarts. That is consistent with everything
  above but has not been caught in the act.

  What the harness does about it now, which is a floor and not a fix: a probe that finds the
  connection refused is retried across the transient budget, and if it is still refused the grid
  restarts the gateway once before writing off the remaining cells (run.rs). So one death costs the
  cells it actually broke rather than every pairing measured after it. If Plano still lands
  partial, the published cells are honest and the absent ones carry the refusal as their reason -
  a gateway that stopped answering is recorded as exactly that, not as a gateway that cannot serve
  the pairing.

  This is deliberately NOT worked around by narrowing Plano's declared matrix. Declaring fewer cells
  to avoid a crash would publish "Plano does not do this" when what happened is "Plano stopped
  running", and those are different claims about someone else's software.
